<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Locarno</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Locarno is a town in the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, on the northern shore of Lake Maggiore. Ruled by the Swiss confederates from 1513, it expelled its Protestant community in 1555, and many of the exiles, including the Orelli and Muralt families, settled in Zürich, where they revived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> industry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locarno-treaties">Locarno Treaties</a> were negotiated there in 1925.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/locarno.jpg" alt="Locarno" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ozonski · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locarno_porto_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Locarno lies at the north-eastern tip of Lake Maggiore, on the north-eastern part of the delta of the river Maggia, at the southern foot of the Swiss Alps. Across the river lies the town of Ascona. Locarno is the capital of its district, and its official language is Italian. It is known for the Locarno International Film Festival, held every August with open-air screenings on the Piazza Grande, and for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locarno-treaties">Locarno Treaties</a>, a series of European territorial agreements negotiated there in October 1925.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Roman tombs found between 1946 and 1949 show that the Roman town lay between the vicus of Muralto and a cemetery in use from the end of the La Tène period to the middle of the 3rd century CE. The place is first mentioned in 807 as Leocarni, and a royal court there is first mentioned in 866. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the town was led by the capitanei, prominent noble families, first named in a document of 1164 in which the Emperor Frederick I granted it market rights.</p><p>The Visconti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> took one of the town's castles in 1342, and it became known as the Visconti Castle. In the early 16th century the Swiss Confederates occupied Locarno, and the twelve cantons took turns to appoint a governor with civil and criminal jurisdiction. They demolished much of the castle in 1531, leaving the core. Under the Confederates Locarno grew into a commercial centre on a road linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> with German-speaking Switzerland.</p><p>By 1535 a Protestant community existed in Locarno, owing its existence to Giovanni Beccaria and refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. After a public debate on 8 May 1549, Locarno declared itself Catholic in 1550, but a large group kept to the Protestant faith. In 1554 the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> gave the Protestants an ultimatum to renounce their faith or go into exile, and on 3 March 1555 more than 100 people left for Zürich. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a> of 1803 created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Canton of Ticino</a> with Locarno as an independent municipality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The sanctuary of Madonna del Sasso above the town, founded after a vision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> experienced by the Franciscan brother Bartolomeo d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivrea">Ivrea</a> in the night of 14 and 15 August 1480, is the main goal of pilgrimage. The Castello Visconteo, which now houses the Archaeological Museum, is one of nine Swiss heritage sites of national significance in the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Locarno” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the sixteenth century Protestant refugees from <b>Locarno</b>, among them the Muralt and Orelli families, revived silk manufacture, and Huguenot refugees later reinforced it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hitler sent soldiers into the demilitarised Rhineland in breach of the Versailles and <b>Locarno</b> treaties.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-7">7 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
